## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of per-tenant privacy controls, simple API design, and ingest-time auditing with a concrete example.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant policy and versioning
- Regex-based redaction and field-level redaction
- Lightweight audit logging without storing raw prompts

## Code Example

```javascript
function sanitizePrompt(tenantPolicy, prompt) {
  let redacted = prompt;
  for (const r of tenantPolicy.redactRegexes) {
    redacted = redacted.replace(new RegExp(r, 'g'), '[REDACTED]');
  }
  tenantPolicy.redactFields.forEach(f => {
    const re = new RegExp(`"${f}"\s*:\s*"[^"]*"`, 'g');
    redacted = redacted.replace(re, `"${f}":"[REDACTED]"`);
  });
  return redacted;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test performance under bursty load?
- How would policy updates drift affect in-flight requests and how would you mitigate?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement cost-aware, latency-conscious routing in a simple, auditable way. Focuses on per-tenant accounting, model pricing, and practical test coverage.

## Key Concepts

- Cost-aware routing and SLA mapping
- Per-tenant ledger and budget enforcement
- Simple fallback behaviors and test plans

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: ledger update and routing decision
function routePrompt(tenant, prompt) {
  const remaining = tenant.budget - tenant.spent;
  for (const m of ['fast','balanced','accurate']) {
    if (cost(m, prompt) <= remaining && latency(m, prompt) <= tenant.sla[m]) {
      tenant.spent += cost(m, prompt);
      return m;
    }
  }
  return 'cached';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to handle budget rollovers and monthly resets?
- How would you test this with A/B experiments across tenants?

